body:
his PR is replacing the draft https://github.com/rhinstaller/kickstart-tests/pull/691 of a GitHub workflow integrating Permian ([kickstart tests plugin](https://github.com/rhinstaller/permian/tree/main/libpermian/plugins/kickstart_test)) into our daily kickstart tests scenario runs. In the end it would replace https://github.com/rhinstaller/kickstart-tests/blob/master/.github/workflows/scenarios.yml.                     
An example run of the workflow on my fork: https://github.com/rvykydal/kickstart-tests/actions/runs/2054657771.
You can see there that after each test a summary/status message is added by Permian workflow (Permian workflow is parsing kickstart-test launcher output to report results of individual tests).

The related Permian kickstart-test plugin PR support was added in https://github.com/rhinstaller/permian/pull/14
The Permian plugin workflow currently [runs launcher](https://github.com/rvykydal/permian/blob/43e598f3bcd21fb463e27ad3164caa1b08b7112d/libpermian/plugins/kickstart_test/__init__.py#L349) locally. The parameters for the launcher come from:
- Test plans: Test case management is done with [tclib](https://github.com/rhinstaller/tclib). In the GitHub workflow test cases are generated from kickstart-tests repository and aggregated into scenario by test plans defining the tests to be run. The [test plans](https://github.com/rvykydal/kickstart-tests/tree/scenarios-permian-workflow-v4-with-url/testlib/test_plans) are generated from templates in the workflow using our [skip-testtypes](https://github.com/rhinstaller/kickstart-tests/blob/master/containers/runner/skip-testtypes) as the single point of truth. In the future the information will be stored in the test plans.
- [Setttings](https://github.com/rvykydal/permian/blob/kstests-daily-scenario-v2/libpermian/plugins/kickstart_test/settings.ini): Settings overrides are passed to permian via `-o` (`--overrides`) option.
- Event: selects the [test plan](https://github.com/rvykydal/kickstart-tests/tree/scenarios-permian-workflow-v4-with-url/testlib/test_plans) by its `type`, launcher `--platform` option by `platform`, and boot.iso and repositories location by `bootIso` and `kstestParams.urls` .

For example:

```
{
  'type' : 'github.scheduled.daily.kstest.rhel8',
  'bootIso': {
    'x84_64': 'file:///boot.updated.with.PR.iso'
  }
  'kstestParams' : {
    'platform' : 'rhel8'
    'urls': {
      x86_64' : {
        'installation_tree': 'http://example.org/the-rhel-9/compose/BaseOS/x86_64/os',
        'modular_url': 'http://example.org/the-rhel-9/compose/AppStream/x86_64/os',
        'ftp_url': 'ftp://example.org/the-rhel-9/compose/BaseOS/x86_64/os',
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Compared to the previous [draft](https://github.com/rhinstaller/kickstart-tests/pull/691) the `kstestParams.urls` is used to set both the boot.iso and the repository location in a single place.

I would propose to schedule the workflow daily on a separate kstest-permian runner to see how it works compared to our current workflow and then update regular kstest runners with permian dependencies (which are not big) https://gitlab.cee.redhat.com/rhinstaller/builders/-/merge_requests/107, turn off the current scenarios.yaml and switch this workflow to use the updated kstest runners.

